          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During pregnancy, cardiac output rises by 30–50%. Which statement correctly desc</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During pregnancy, cardiac output rises by 30–50%. Which statement correctly describes the temporal pattern of this increase?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Heart rate rises sharply in the first trimester and accounts for most of the early increase in cardiac output</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Heart rate rises progressively throughout pregnancy, not sharply in the first trimester. The early CO increase is driven mainly by stroke volume.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Stroke volume peaks in the second trimester, while heart rate increases progressively to term</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — stroke volume rises first (10–15%, driven by plasma volume expansion) and plateaus mid-pregnancy, while heart rate climbs steadily by 10–20 bpm to become the dominant contributor by term.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Both stroke volume and heart rate peak simultaneously in the third trimester</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Stroke volume peaks earlier, in the second trimester. Only heart rate continues to rise through the third trimester.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cardiac output peaks in early pregnancy and gradually returns towards baseline by term</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cardiac output does not return to baseline. It peaks at 28–32 weeks and remains elevated until delivery, with a further surge during labour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Stroke volume increases first and peaks in the second trimester (driven by expanded plasma volume). Heart rate rises progressively to term (10–20 bpm above baseline), becoming the main contributor to cardiac output in late pregnancy."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Stroke volume rises early (plasma volume expansion → increased preload) and peaks by the second trimester. Heart rate then takes over, rising 10–20 bpm progressively to term."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
