          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During a bimanual pelvic examination, a gynaecologist palpates a firm nodular ma</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During a bimanual pelvic examination, a gynaecologist palpates a firm nodular mass through the posterior fornix. Which structure lies immediately behind the posterior fornix?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urinary bladder</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The bladder lies anterior to the uterus and is related to the anterior fornix, not the posterior fornix.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pouch of Douglas (rectouterine pouch)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — the posterior fornix is separated from the Pouch of Douglas by only a thin peritoneal wall, making it the key access point for detecting pathology such as endometriosis or metastatic deposits.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Broad ligament</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The broad ligament extends laterally from the uterus towards the pelvic side wall. It is not directly behind the posterior fornix.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Uterosacral ligament</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The uterosacral ligaments run posteriorly from the cervix to the sacrum. While endometriosis nodules on them may be felt, the structure immediately behind the posterior fornix is the Pouch of Douglas itself.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The Pouch of Douglas (rectouterine pouch) lies directly behind the posterior fornix, separated by only a thin peritoneal wall. Induration palpated here during a bimanual examination suggests endometriosis on the uterosacral ligaments or metastatic peritoneal disease."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The Pouch of Douglas is the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity and is separated from the posterior fornix by only a thin wall — explaining why fluid, endometriotic deposits, or tumour seedlings can be felt there."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
